Named Entity Recognition

Named Entity is anything that can be referred to as a proper name
    proper name is a full phrase, 4 tags most commeon
    PER (person) Marie Curie
    LOC (location) New York City
    GPE (geo-political entity) Boulder, Colorado
also extended to
    dates, times, prices

Task : find and tag the type / NER Named Entity Tagging, it is a first step in
    - sentiment analysis
    - question answering
    - information extraction

Why NER is had
1. Segmentation : find a span of text, and where the boundaries are
2. Type ambiguity, e.g. Washington : location, name?

BIO tagging
    B : token that begins a span
    I : token inside a span
    O : tokens outside of any span

Variance
    IO labels (no B)
    BIO labels
    BIOES : begin, inside or end of span

Words classes, PoS tags
       - noun : houses, cat, Anna
            concrete or abstract
            proper or common
        - verb : play, write
            main (eat)
            auxiliary (can)
        (- adhectives : property)
        - pronoun : he, she, it / closed
        - preprostion : by, in
        - adverb : carefully
        - conjunction : and, but / closed
        (- determiners : the some / closed)
        (- prepositions : to with / closed)
        - particles : off up / closed
        - article : a, an, the
        (- numbers : 0, one)

        q : no adjective?

Closed class words
    - relatively fixed membership
    - prepositions : new are rarely coined
    - usually function words

Open class words
    - nouns > new like iPhone
    - usually content words : nouns, verbs, adjectives
        adverbs
        plus intersections > oh, ouch..

Part-of-Speech Tagging
    roughly 15% are ambiguous, very common
    around 60% of word tokens are ambiguous

- prior probability : of 'will' is usually AUX
- identity of neighbouring words : 'the' means the next word is probably not a verb
- morphology and word shape : un - > ADJ

Words vary along 3 affective dimensions (NRC VAD lexicon)
    - valence : the pleasantness of the stimulus
    - arousal : the intensity of emotion provoked by stimulus
    - dominance : the degree of control exerted by the stimulus

3 affective dimensions for a word
    - a point in space
    - each word is a vector
    - similar words are near

   embedding (vectors) - to represent a word meaning

Why vectors? - we can generalize to similar but unseen words

tf-idf
    - sparse
    - a common baseline model
    - words are represented by the counts of nearby vectors

Word2vec
    - dense
    - representation created by training to predict if the word is likely to appear
    - contextual embedding

Word-word matrix (or term context matrix)
two words are similar in meaning if their context vectors are similar
    - n of times appear in the same document
    - (smaller scale) some n to the right and to the left
        plus or minis forward 4 words window

Cosine of the angle btw two vectors
- dot product (inner product)
- dot product tends to be high when the two vectors have
large values in the same dimensions

- favors long vectors
    - higher when vector is longer
    - normalize by dividing by the length

It is the cos of the angle btw
   from 1 (vectors pointng to same direction)
   to -1 (opposite),
   frequencies are not negative, so cos is from 0 to 1

Raw frequency is bad
    - tf idf / when dimensions are documents
        w = tf * idf

        tf - term frequency, not raw
        tf = log10(count (t,d) + 1)
        adding 1 because cannot take a log of zero

        df - number of documents where t occurs.
        collection frequency - how many times occurs
            in the entre collection, Romeo : 113
        document frequency - in how many documents
            Romeo : 1

        idf = log10(N/df), N / total number of docs
            in the collection
            df / number of docs where the word occurs

        idf - inverse document frequency to down weight
            words like 'the' or 'it'

    - PMI / point wise mutual information
        when dimensions are words
        PMI (w1, w2) = log ( p(w1,w2) / p(w1) p(w2))
        compares the probabilities we see with
        what we would have expected

What is a document?
    - anything, like paragraph

Word2vec
    embeddings, short dense vectors
    tf-idf (or PMI) vectors are
        long (length |V| = 20,000 to 50,000)
        sparse (most elements are zero)

    learn vectors which are
        short (length 50-1000)
        dense (most elements are non zero)

dense vector works better than sparse
    - classifier learn / tune fewer weights
    - may generalize better
    - capture synonymy better

Compute Word2vec using skipgram / static embedding
    also referred as word2vec

Dynamic embedding / diff in diff context

approach : predict if candidate word c is a neighbor
1. treat the target word t and a neighbouring context word c as positive examples
2. randomly sample other words in the lexicon to get negative examples
3. use logistic regression to distinguish cases
4. use the learned weights as embedding of words

Similarity(w,c) apr. w*c
use sigma to probability, need to normalize to turn
the similarity (numbers, potentially negative) to
probability

P(+|w,c) = sigma (c*w) = 1 / (1 + exp (-c*w))
P(-|w,c) = 1 - P(+|w,c) = sigma (-c*w) = 1 / (1 + exp (c*w))

P(+|w,c) : c is a context word

Skip-gram assumption
    all context words are independent
    the probability to multiply

    P(+|w,c1:L) = MULT sigma (ci * w)
    log P(+|w,c1:L) = MULT log sigma (ci * w)

Skipgram
    - a probabilistic classifier, given
    a test target word w
    its context window of L words c1:L

    - estimates probability that w occurs in
    this window based on similarity of w
    (embeddings) to c1:L embeddings()

    need embedding for all words in vocabulary

store two embeddings
    - W target words
    - C context words

For each positive example, we will use k negative examples.
    noise word - a random word from the lexicon that is not a target word w

Small window (c = +-2 ) nearest words are syntactically similar
Large windows (c = +-5) words in the same semantic field

